---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import { caseStudies } from '../../lib/projects';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

const project = caseStudies[2];

const sections = [
  { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
  { id: 'ballot', label: 'The ballot flow' },
  { id: 'power', label: 'The brownout problem' },
  { id: 'cloud', label: 'The cloud path' },
  { id: 'status', label: 'Where it stands' },
];
---
<CaseLayout project={project} sections={sections} prev={{ slug: 'attendx', title: 'AttendX' }} next={{ slug: 'swarm', title: 'Swarm robotics' }}>
  <!-- 01 overview -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="overview">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">01</span><span class="rail__label">Overview</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      A biometric voting terminal: one ESP32, one voter at a time. A vote only exists after an
      <strong>AS608 optical fingerprint match</strong>. Twenty political party choices sit behind a
      paginated 16×4 LCD and 4×4 keypad, and every verified ballot is pushed over Wi-Fi to a
      Render-hosted dashboard backed by Neon PostgreSQL, so the tally updates live while voting is
      still happening.
    </p>
    <dl class="kv" data-reveal>
      <dt>Why biometric</dt><dd>One fingerprint, one vote. The module's template matching is the eligibility gate, and it works offline.</dd>
      <dt>Why the cloud</dt><dd>Collation is where paper systems fail. Writing each ballot straight to a hosted database removes the transcription step entirely.</dd>
      <dt>The hard part</dt><dd>Not the voting logic. It was keeping an optical scanner alive on the same rail as Wi-Fi and an LCD.</dd>
    </dl>
    <figure class="figure figure--wide" data-reveal>
      <div class="figure__frame"><img src={url('/img/evoting/terminal.jpg')} alt="The e-voting terminal in its enclosure: 16x4 LCD at voter-ID entry, 4x4 keypad, green button lit" loading="lazy" /></div>
      <figcaption>THE TERMINAL · E-VOTE NIGERIA at the identify step · the [OFFLINE] banner is the terminal reporting its own link state</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 ballot flow -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="ballot">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">02</span><span class="rail__label">The ballot flow</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <ol class="chain" data-reveal>
      <li><span class="mono chain__k">identify</span><span>Voter presents a finger; the AS608 optical module matches against enrolled templates on-module.</span></li>
      <li><span class="mono chain__k">offer</span><span>Twenty parties, paginated across the 16×4 LCD, four at a time, chosen with the 4×4 keypad. Pagination beats scrolling: the whole option list stays legible at arm's length.</span></li>
      <li><span class="mono chain__k">confirm</span><span>One confirm step before the ballot is cast, so a mis-key can be recovered without inviting double-voting.</span></li>
      <li><span class="mono chain__k">record</span><span>The ballot goes out over Wi-Fi to the dashboard; the terminal returns to the identify state immediately.</span></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      The state machine is deliberately boring: identify, offer, confirm, record, with one way in
      and one way out of each state. Election equipment should be dull.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 power -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="power">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">03</span><span class="rail__label">The brownout problem</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      The AS608's optical scan draws a burst of current at exactly the moment the ESP32 is pushing
      a Wi-Fi frame. On a shared, loosely-decoupled rail the result is a voltage dip during the
      scan, and the MCU resets mid-ballot. A terminal that reboots while someone is voting is not
      a prototype problem; it is the whole product failing at its one job.
    </p>
    <div class="note" data-reveal>
      <p class="note__title">Fix</p>
      <p><strong>Decoupling capacitors placed for the scan's current burst</strong>, local charge
      storage at the sensor so the dip never reaches the ESP32's brownout detector. Scans stopped
      resetting the board, and the confirm step stopped doubling as a crash reporter.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      This project is where power stopped being an afterthought to me: the schematic was fine, the
      code was fine, and the system still failed until the rail was fixed. I have budgeted decoupling
      into every build since.
    </p>
    <figure class="figure figure--wide" data-reveal>
      <div class="figure__frame"><img src={url('/img/evoting/build.jpg')} alt="The e-voting rig on veroboard: ESP32, AS608 optical sensor and 16x4 LCD on the bench beside a laptop" loading="lazy" /></div>
      <figcaption>THE RIG · ESP32, AS608 and LCD on veroboard, the rail that browned out under that red LED</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 cloud -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="cloud">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">04</span><span class="rail__label">The cloud path</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <dl class="kv" data-reveal>
      <dt>Terminal → cloud</dt><dd>Ballots POST from the ESP32 over Wi-Fi, one HTTP request per verified vote.</dd>
      <dt>Backend</dt><dd>Neon PostgreSQL (serverless Postgres, free tier) behind a dashboard hosted on Render, also free tier.</dd>
      <dt>Dashboard</dt><dd>Live tallies per party, updating as votes land; the collation centre reads the same table the terminal writes.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      The whole pipeline runs on free tiers, end to end. That constraint shaped the design in
      useful ways: one request per ballot, no streaming sockets to hold open, and a schema simple
      enough to read at a glance.
    </p>
    <figure class="figure figure--wide" data-reveal>
      <div class="figure__frame"><img src={url('/img/evoting/dashboard.png')} alt="The e-vote admin dashboard: election state, turnout progress, accepted ballots and terminal telemetry" loading="lazy" /></div>
      <figcaption>THE RECORD · the admin dashboard over Neon Postgres, from a demo session (one ballot cast, election open)</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- 05 status -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="status">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">05</span><span class="rail__label">Where it stands</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <div class="stats" data-reveal>
      <div class="stat"><p class="stat__value tnum">20</p><p class="stat__label">Party choices behind fingerprint + keypad, paginated</p></div>
      <div class="stat stat--green"><p class="stat__value tnum">0</p><p class="stat__label">Resets during optical scans after the decoupling fix</p></div>
      <div class="stat"><p class="stat__value tnum">2</p><p class="stat__label">Free-tier services carrying the live tally (Neon, Render)</p></div>
      <div class="stat stat--amber"><p class="stat__value tnum">demo</p><p class="stat__label">Status: working demonstration build, not fielded equipment</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      Honest scope: this is a working demonstration of the architecture (biometric gate, paginated
      ballot, live cloud tally), not certified election equipment. What it proves is the part I
      own: hardware bring-up, power design, and a firmware state machine that treats a reboot
      mid-ballot as a defect.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>

<style>
  .chain { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .chain li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 150px minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 1.25rem; padding-block: 0.85rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line-soft); color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.93rem; }
  .chain li:last-child { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-soft); }
  .chain__k { color: var(--green); padding-top: 0.15rem; white-space: nowrap; }
  .note { margin-top: 1.5rem; }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .chain li { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.2rem; }
  }
</style>
